          "text": "{\"translation_en\":null,\"larger_context_summary\":\"Late in the novel, Amy March is speaking privately with Laurie, now her fiancé, after their friendship in Europe has developed into love and an engagement. Laurie’s wealth creates a possible doubt about Amy’s motives, especially because she has long understood marriage as carrying economic as well as romantic consequences. Here she firmly reassures him that money did not determine her acceptance: she says she had momentarily forgotten his fortune when she agreed to marry him and would have chosen him even in poverty. The narrator’s playful reference to Amy’s private affection indicates that her actions immediately confirm her words. The exchange helps establish their relationship as sincere rather than merely advantageous before they return to the March family as a couple.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The target clause is grammatically embedded in Amy’s wish that Laurie were poor so that she could demonstrate the extent of her love. That embedding matters: she is not offering an isolated formula but presenting her love as a real, enduring state that explains a counterfactual willingness to marry him penniless. The narrator then explicitly treats her words as true and says her affectionate conduct supplies convincing proof, making the truth-bearing force especially strong. There is also a limited commitment dimension because the avowal supports her acceptance of Laurie and her readiness to share a life unaffected by his fortune. Yet the actual undertaking belongs chiefly to “I’d have married you” and the surrounding engagement, not independently to the embedded words. Although the scene is emotionally warm and “how much” intensifies the claimed feeling, intensity is not the same as a spontaneous or reflexive expressive discharge; Amy’s reassurance is deliberate and argument-like.\"}"
        }
